      <p class="content-hero-updated">Updated &middot; February 2025</p>
      <h1 class="content-hero-title">Ayahuasca</h1>
      <p class="content-hero-subtitle">Ayahuasca is a psychoactive brew prepared from the Banisteriopsis caapi vine and DMT-containing plants, most commonly Psychotria viridis. It is a living ceremonial tradition, a powerful healing tool, and one of the most pharmacologically complex psychedelic substances in use. This guide covers what ayahuasca is, how it works, what the experience involves, the critical safety considerations, and the ethical dimensions of non-Indigenous engagement.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- At a Glance HUD -->
    <SubstanceHud id="ayahuasca" />

    <!-- Table of Contents (sidebar on desktop) -->
    <div class="article-layout">
      <SubstanceNav id="ayahuasca" />

      <div class="article-body">

        <!-- What Ayahuasca Is -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-what">What Ayahuasca Is</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca (also known as yag&eacute;, hoasca, daime, and by dozens of other regional names) is a psychoactive brew prepared from the <em>Banisteriopsis caapi</em> vine (the ayahuasca vine itself) and the leaves of <em>Psychotria viridis</em> (chacruna) or other DMT-containing plants. The vine contains beta-carboline alkaloids (primarily harmine, harmaline, and tetrahydroharmine) which are reversible monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs). These alkaloids inhibit the enzyme that would otherwise break down DMT in the digestive system before it reaches the bloodstream, allowing oral DMT to become psychoactive.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The brew has been used by Indigenous peoples of the Amazon basin (including the Shipibo-Conibo, Shuar, Yawanaw&aacute;, and dozens of other nations) for centuries as a healing, divination, and ceremonial tool. Ayahuasca is not a recreational drug in its original context; it is a medicine administered by trained healers within carefully maintained ceremonial structures.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The brew is now used globally, in Indigenous and neo-Indigenous ceremonies, in syncretic religious traditions (Santo Daime, Uni&atilde;o do Vegetal), in retreat settings, and informally by individuals who prepare it themselves. This global diffusion has created significant ethical, cultural, and safety tensions that anyone engaging with ayahuasca should understand.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Where It Comes From -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-sources">Where Ayahuasca Comes From</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca is at minimum two plants, and which two matters more than most people are told. The vine <em>Banisteriopsis caapi</em> supplies the beta-carbolines that make the brew work orally. A second plant supplies the DMT. Across most of the Peruvian and Brazilian Amazon that plant is <em>Psychotria viridis</em>, called chacruna. In Ecuador and parts of Colombia it is more often <em>Diplopterys cabrerana</em>, known as chaliponga or chagropanga, a different leaf with a different alkaloid balance and a reputation for longer and more visually saturated brews. Both are served under the same name.</p>
          <p class="content-p">There is also a third possibility that surprises people. In parts of Colombia and in some vegetalista lineages, ayahuasca means the vine alone, with no DMT plant added at all. Vine-only brews are slower, more physical, and far less visionary. Someone who has drunk a vine-only preparation and someone who has drunk a strong chacruna brew have not had the same experience, and neither of them was given the wrong thing.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Beyond the two core plants, many lineages add others. Tobacco, <em>Brugmansia</em> (to&eacute;), and a range of regional admixtures all change what the brew does, sometimes substantially. Those additions are a safety question rather than a botanical curiosity, and they are covered in <a href="#aya-testing" class="inline-link">verifying what you have</a>.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Preparation is a long reduction rather than a chemical extraction. The vine is pounded and boiled with the leaves for hours, the liquid is drawn off, and the process is often repeated over one or more days until what remains is a thick dark liquid. Nothing in that process is standardised. The strength of the final brew is a property of the particular pot, which is the single fact that shapes the <a href="#aya-dosage" class="inline-link">dosage section below</a>.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Harvest pressure on the vine</h3>
          <p class="content-p"><em>B. caapi</em> is a slow-growing woody liana, and traditional practice favours mature vine, often decades old. Retreat demand has concentrated harvesting around the towns that host it, and vine is now routinely brought in from further away at higher cost. Cultivation is expanding and is the obvious answer, but a planted vine takes years to reach a usable size, so supply lags demand by roughly the length of time it takes to grow one. Asking where a centre's vine comes from is a reasonable question, and a good centre will have an answer.</p>
          <NatureCard id="ayahuasca" />
        </div>

        <!-- History & Cultural Roots -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-history">History &amp; Cultural Roots</h2>
          <p class="content-p">The oldest direct chemical evidence of an ayahuasca-like preparation is not from the Amazon. A ritual bundle recovered from a rock shelter in the Bolivian Andes, radiocarbon dated to roughly a thousand years ago, tested positive for harmine and DMT together, alongside several other psychoactive plants. Whatever else that tells us, it means the practice of combining these two alkaloid classes is old and was not confined to the lowland forest.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The written record starts much later and starts with outsiders. The Ecuadorian geographer Manuel Villavicencio described drinking the brew in 1858. The British botanist Richard Spruce had collected and identified the vine on the Rio Uaup&eacute;s several years earlier, though his account was not published until 1908. When chemists began isolating the vine's main alkaloid in the early twentieth century they named it three separate times, once as telepathine, on the assumption that it produced telepathy. It was eventually shown to be harmine, a compound already known from Syrian rue.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Indigenous use across the Amazon basin is not one tradition. The Shipibo-Conibo, Shuar, Yawanaw&aacute;, Ash&aacute;ninka, Tukano and dozens of other nations use the brew with different songs, different admixtures, different cosmologies, and different rules about who may serve it. Treating these as one practice is the most common mistake Western writing makes about ayahuasca.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Most Westerners do not encounter any of them directly. What they encounter is <em>vegetalismo</em>, the mestizo healing tradition of the Peruvian Amazon that developed out of Indigenous practice, missionary contact, and river-town trade. Vegetalismo is a real lineage with real training, and it is the idiom most retreat ceremonies are conducted in. It is worth knowing which one you are in.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The Brazilian churches</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Three syncretic religions formed around the brew in twentieth-century Brazil, none of them Indigenous and all of them urban in reach. Santo Daime was founded by Raimundo Irineu Serra in Acre in the 1930s. Barquinha followed in 1945, and Uni&atilde;o do Vegetal was founded by Jos&eacute; Gabriel da Costa in 1961. They matter to a reader for a practical reason as much as a historical one. Nearly every place where ayahuasca has any legal standing today, it has it because one of these churches went to court.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The export of ceremony to Europe and North America began in earnest in the 1990s and accelerated through the 2000s, first through the churches and then through a retreat industry that now operates at a scale no traditional practice was built for. The consequences of that shift are the subject of the <a href="#aya-ethics" class="inline-link">ethics section</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Ceremony and Container -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-ceremony">Ceremony and Container</h2>
          <p class="content-p">In Indigenous and neo-Indigenous contexts, ayahuasca is administered within a ceremonial structure that has been refined over generations. The ceremony provides the container within which the experience unfolds, including the icaros (healing songs) sung by the curandero or curandera, the physical space (maloca), and the relationships of accountability between healer, community, and participant.</p>
          <p class="content-p">This ceremonial architecture is not decorative. Research by anthropologists and clinicians alike suggests that the ceremonial container is a significant determinant of the quality and character of the experience, consistent with the set-and-setting principle operating at a collective rather than individual level.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The practical consequence is that choosing a ceremony is choosing a container, and the questions worth asking before you commit are set out in <a href="#aya-considering" class="inline-link">if you are considering this</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Ethics, Sovereignty, and Informed Engagement -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-ethics">Ethics, Sovereignty, and Informed Engagement</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca presents some of the most significant ethical challenges in contemporary psychedelic practice. The global spread of ayahuasca has generated substantial revenue for Western retreat operators, while the Amazonian communities who developed and transmitted this knowledge frequently receive little benefit and face ongoing threats to their territories, cultural sovereignty, and linguistic survival.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Researchers Yuria Celidwen, Nicole Redvers, and others have articulated frameworks for ethical non-Indigenous engagement: genuine relationship with and compensation for Indigenous practitioners, refusal to participate in extractive tourism models, support for Indigenous land rights, and epistemic humility about what one does and does not understand about the tradition being engaged with.</p>
          <p class="content-p">None of this prohibits non-Indigenous people from working with ayahuasca. It does mean that doing so thoughtfully requires engagement with these questions rather than consumption of a product.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Pharmacology -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-pharmacology">Pharmacology: How Ayahuasca Works</h2>
          <p class="content-p">The active psychedelic compound in ayahuasca is N,N-dimethyltryptamine (DMT), a tryptamine that activates serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, the primary target of all classical psychedelics. DMT is also endogenous in humans; it is found in trace amounts in human blood, urine, and cerebrospinal fluid, though its physiological function is not yet well understood.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Ordinarily, DMT taken orally is rapidly degraded by monoamine oxidase (MAO) enzymes in the gut and liver before it can cross the blood-brain barrier. The beta-carboline alkaloids in <em>B. caapi</em> inhibit MAO, allowing oral DMT to become bioavailable. Combining a psychedelic compound with its own enzyme inhibitor in a single brew is one of the most sophisticated examples of traditional botanical pharmacology known.</p>
          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="callout-label">MAOI interactions. This is critical</div>
            <p>Because ayahuasca contains MAOIs, it has the potential for serious and life-threatening interactions with a wide range of substances and medications. This is the single most important safety consideration for anyone considering ayahuasca. See <a href="#aya-risks" class="inline-link">MAOI interactions</a> for the full list.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Effects and Experience -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-effects">Effects and Experience</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca ceremonies typically take place at night and last four to six hours. The brew is bitter and frequently produces nausea and vomiting, referred to in many traditions as "la purga" (the purge), understood not merely as a physical response but as a process of emotional and energetic release. Diarrhoea is also common.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Onset and arc</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Effects begin 20&ndash;60 minutes after ingestion and reach peak intensity between 1&ndash;3 hours. The experience often involves multiple waves of intensity rather than a single continuous arc, and additional doses (called "medicina" or top-ups) are sometimes offered by the facilitating healer mid-ceremony.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Phenomenological character</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca experiences are often described as more emotionally and psychologically directive than other psychedelics, more likely to produce confrontational encounters with psychological shadow material, relational trauma, or existential themes. Visions are common: complex geometric and figurative imagery, encounters with animals and beings, and narrative sequences that practitioners frequently experience as meaningful communications rather than random hallucinations.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Experiences of connection to the natural world, encounters with plant intelligence, and a felt sense of ecological embeddedness are among the most frequently reported themes in both qualitative research and practitioner accounts, making ayahuasca one of the most ecologically rich of all psychedelic substances from a phenomenological standpoint.</p>

          <div class="content-callout">
            <div class="callout-label">Psygaia Framework</div>
            <p>The recurrence of nature-based and ecological themes in ayahuasca phenomenology, particularly the quality of plant intelligence and interspecies communication, is a central interest of the <a href="https://psygaia.org/psygaia-hypothesis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Psygaia Framework</a>, which offers a biosemiotic and enactive interpretation of these experiences without recourse to literal metaphysical claims.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Dosage -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-dosage">Dosage and Method</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca cannot be dosed by volume, and that is the most practically important thing to understand about it. Published analyses of brews have found the DMT content of a standard serving varying by roughly an order of magnitude between samples that looked and tasted much the same. Two cups of the same size, poured from two different pots, are not comparable measures of anything.</p>
          <p class="content-p">This has a consequence that separates ayahuasca from every other substance covered on this site. With LSD or MDMA the reader decides the dose. With ayahuasca the reader delegates it, to whoever brewed the liquid and whoever pours it. Everything in the <a href="#aya-considering" class="inline-link">considering</a> section about vetting a facilitator follows from this one fact.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">What a serving looks like</h3>
          <p class="content-p">A cup is typically 50 to 150 ml of thick, bitter, dark liquid. Analysed brews have generally fallen somewhere around 20 to 60 mg of DMT per serving with a comparable or higher load of beta-carbolines, but those figures describe the samples that happened to be tested rather than the cup in front of you. Common ceremonial practice is to pour a conservative first cup and offer a second an hour or two later, judged by how the person appears to be doing. That second offer is a real decision point and declining it is normal.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The only oral route is drinking it, and the brew has no meaningful alternative route. The beta-carbolines exist to protect DMT from digestion, so the oral path is the entire point of the preparation.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Analogue preparations</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Some people combine a measured quantity of extracted DMT with a measured beta-carboline source, usually harmala alkaloids from Syrian rue, in what is generally called pharmahuasca. This is the one version that can be dosed by number. It trades an unknown concentration for complete personal responsibility for an MAOI, which is a serious trade rather than a simplification. Getting the beta-carboline dose wrong is the failure mode, and the <a href="#aya-risks" class="inline-link">interaction risks below</a> apply in full.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Food restriction before a ceremony is partly pharmacological rather than purely traditional. Aged, cured, and fermented foods carry tyramine, which an MAOI stops the body from clearing normally. That distinction is worth knowing, and it is explained in <a href="#aya-considering" class="inline-link">the dieta</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Research -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-research">Research on Ayahuasca</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Clinical research on ayahuasca has expanded significantly in the past decade, driven primarily by Brazilian and Spanish research groups. Key findings include:</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Depression.</strong> A randomised controlled trial by Palhano-Fontes and colleagues (2019, Psychological Medicine) found significant antidepressant effects from a single ayahuasca session in treatment-resistant patients, with effects persisting at one and seven days. Effect sizes were large.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Neuroimaging.</strong> Studies show ayahuasca produces significant changes in default mode network connectivity, consistent with psilocybin findings. DMT specifically has been associated with a dramatic increase in neural complexity.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Nature-connectedness and ecological values.</strong> Qualitative and survey research consistently finds that ayahuasca experiences are associated with increased sense of connection to nature and motivation for pro-environmental behaviour, among the strongest effects in the psychedelic-ecology literature.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Risks and Contraindications -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-risks">Risks and Contraindications</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca carries the psychological contraindications common to all classical psychedelics, plus one category that is genuinely its own. The MAOI content creates interaction risks that do not apply to psilocybin or LSD, and some of those interactions can kill. Read this section before you read anything else on the page about preparation.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">MAOI interactions</h3>
          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="callout-label">Ayahuasca carries unique interaction risks</div>
            <p>The MAOI content of ayahuasca creates potentially fatal interactions with SSRIs, stimulants, and many common medications. This is not a standard psychedelic precaution, these interactions are medically serious. <a href="/assessment/#ass-combochart" class="inline-link">View full drug interaction chart &rarr;</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="dosage-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Substance / Class</th><th>Risk</th><th>Level</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>SSRIs and SNRIs</strong></td><td>Risk of serotonin syndrome (potentially fatal): hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, seizures</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>MAOIs (pharmaceutical)</strong></td><td>Combined MAOI load; extreme potentiation and cardiovascular risk</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong></td><td>Serotonin syndrome risk</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Tramadol</strong></td><td>Serotonergic; significant serotonin syndrome risk with MAOIs</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Dextromethorphan (DXM)</strong></td><td>Found in many cough medicines; serious serotonin syndrome risk</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Stimulants (amphetamine, cocaine)</strong></td><td>Hypertensive crisis with MAOIs</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Lithium</strong></td><td>Seizure risk</td><td><span class="risk-high">Do not combine</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Tyramine-rich foods</strong></td><td>Cheese, aged meats, fermented foods: hypertensive crisis potential</td><td><span class="risk-mod">Avoid for 24hrs before/after</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Cannabis</strong></td><td>Significant intensification; anxiety amplification</td><td><span class="risk-mod">Use with extreme caution</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="content-p">Allow a minimum of two weeks after stopping SSRIs before drinking ayahuasca, and only do so under medical guidance. Fluoxetine (Prozac) has a longer half-life and requires at least five weeks.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Psychological and medical contraindications</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Beyond MAOI interactions, ayahuasca carries the same psychological contraindications as other classical psychedelics: personal or family history of schizophrenia spectrum disorders or bipolar I is a firm contraindication. Cardiovascular conditions warrant medical consultation given blood pressure fluctuations. Ayahuasca is not appropriate during pregnancy.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The intensity and duration of ayahuasca experiences, typically longer and more emotionally confrontational than psilocybin, means that psychological preparation is especially important. Attempting ayahuasca without adequate preparation, experienced facilitation, and a supported integration plan is inadvisable regardless of prior experience with other psychedelics.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Verifying What You Have -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-testing">Verifying What You Have</h2>
          <p class="content-p">The honest answer is that you mostly cannot. Reagent kits are designed to produce a readable colour change from a small amount of a solid, largely single compound. Ayahuasca is a dark, opaque liquid containing several alkaloids at once, and a reagent drop into it tells you nothing you can act on. There is no home method that will give you the DMT content of the cup you are about to drink, which is exactly why the <a href="#aya-dosage" class="inline-link">dosage section</a> says what it says.</p>
          <p class="content-p">So verification here means verifying plants and people rather than chemistry. Four questions do most of the work, and a facilitator who cannot answer them has told you something useful.</p>
          <ul class="content-list">
            <li><strong>Which DMT plant is in it?</strong> Chacruna and chaliponga produce noticeably different experiences, and chaliponga brews tend to run longer. Knowing which one you are drinking lets you plan the night realistically.</li>
            <li><strong>Is there to&eacute; in the brew?</strong> <em>Brugmansia</em> is a tropane deliriant rather than a psychedelic. It produces confusion, amnesia, and at higher doses genuine medical danger, and it is sometimes added without participants being told. This is the single most important question on the list. Ask it directly and expect a direct answer.</li>
            <li><strong>Is tobacco involved, and where?</strong> Mapacho (<em>Nicotiana rustica</em>) is used legitimately in many lineages. Concentrated tobacco preparations taken as purges are a separate practice with their own history of serious harm, including deaths. If a retreat includes them, treat that as its own decision rather than part of the ayahuasca decision.</li>
            <li><strong>Who brewed this, and when?</strong> Brews are made in batches and kept. The person pouring may not be the person who cooked it, and strength varies from pot to pot regardless.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="content-p">If you are sourcing plants yourself, identification is the entire problem. Material sold online as chacruna is frequently something else, and the vine is not the part people usually get wrong. Buying from a vendor who cannot name the species and the origin is buying an unknown.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Legal Status -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-legal">Legal Status</h2>
          <p class="content-p">DMT is a Schedule I substance under the 1971 UN Convention. The plants that contain it are not, because the Convention controls substances rather than the organisms that produce them, and the International Narcotics Control Board has acknowledged that gap directly. National law generally closes it by treating a prepared brew as a preparation of DMT. The practical upshot in most countries is that the living plants are legal to own and the liquid is not.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Brazil:</strong> religious use has been protected since a federal decision in 1987, reaffirmed by CONAD in 2010. This is the most settled legal position anywhere.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Peru:</strong> ceremonial use is legal, and in 2008 the National Institute of Culture declared traditional ayahuasca practice part of the national cultural heritage.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>United States:</strong> Schedule I as a DMT preparation. Two religious exemptions exist and both had to be won in court. The Uni&atilde;o do Vegetal prevailed unanimously at the Supreme Court in 2006 under the Religious Freedom Restoration Act, and a Santo Daime church in Oregon won in federal court in 2009. There is no application process and no general exemption. Those rulings cover those organisations.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Canada:</strong> Health Canada has issued Section 56 exemptions to a small number of Santo Daime and UDV congregations since 2017, on a case-by-case basis.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Netherlands:</strong> tolerated for years, then not. The Dutch Supreme Court ruled in 2019 that religious use does not exempt ayahuasca from the Opium Act.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Almost everywhere else it is illegal, with penalties tracking whatever DMT carries locally. One point matters specifically to travellers: because ayahuasca is a heavy liquid, jurisdictions that sentence by total preparation weight rather than by active-compound content can treat a few litres as a trafficking quantity. Carrying brew across a border is a materially different legal risk from drinking it at a retreat. See the <a href="/law/#law-table" class="inline-link">legal quick reference</a> for how this compares to other substances.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- If You're Considering This -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-considering">If You Are Considering This</h2>
          <p class="content-p">The general groundwork is covered elsewhere on this site: <a href="/assessment/" class="inline-link">assessment</a> for whether this is a reasonable thing for you to do at all, <a href="/preparation/" class="inline-link">preparation</a> for how to get ready, and <a href="/integration/" class="inline-link">integration</a> for afterwards. Four things are specific to ayahuasca and are not covered there.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The medication washout is long and it is not optional</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Two weeks off most SSRIs, five for fluoxetine, and both figures are minimums. Stopping an antidepressant is a medical decision that belongs with a prescriber, not something to arrange quietly around one because a retreat is already booked. If you are not in a position to come off a serotonergic medication safely, that is a reason not to drink rather than a scheduling problem. The <a href="#aya-risks" class="inline-link">interaction table</a> covers what is at stake.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The dieta is two different things at once</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Part of it is pharmacology. Avoiding aged cheese, cured meat, fermented foods, and alcohol matters because an MAOI stops the body clearing tyramine normally, and the result can be a sharp rise in blood pressure. Part of it is tradition: abstaining from sex, sugar, salt, or pork carries meaning within a given lineage and has no equivalent mechanism behind it. Both are worth following if you have committed to a ceremony, and knowing which is which keeps you from dismissing the whole thing or from treating all of it as medicine.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">You are choosing a person, not just a substance</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Because the dose is decided for you, vetting matters more here than anywhere else on this site. Worth asking before you pay: what lineage and training does the person serving have, and who vouches for it? How many people will be in the room, and will a sober person be present all night? What is the plan if someone needs a hospital, and how far away is one? What preparation and follow-up are included? And do they ask about your medications and psychiatric history before they take your money? A centre that never asks has told you what kind of operation it is.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Plan the landing, not just the flight</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca is usually taken in retreat blocks, often abroad, and people frequently fly home within a day or two of the last ceremony. That is the worst possible moment to have nothing arranged. Leave a clear week before anything demanding, and decide who you will talk to before you go rather than after you get back.</p>

          <p class="content-p">Ayahuasca asks for more logistics than any other substance covered here, and most of what goes wrong with it goes wrong in the arrangements rather than in the experience. Sort the arrangements first.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="aya-faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-section">

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question"><span>How do I prepare for an ayahuasca ceremony?</span><span class="faq-icon">+</span></div>
              <div class="faq-answer"><p>Most ceremonial traditions recommend a dieta, a preparatory period involving dietary restrictions (no pork, alcohol, fermented foods, excessive sugar or salt), reduced media consumption, sexual abstinence, and quiet reflection. The length varies by tradition, from three days to two weeks or more. The dieta is understood as a preparation not just of the body but of intention and openness.</p></div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question"><span>What is "la purga" (the purge)?</span><span class="faq-icon">+</span></div>
              <div class="faq-answer"><p>Nausea and vomiting are common, particularly during the first few hours. Traditional practitioners understand purging as part of the healing process, a release of accumulated psychic and physical material, rather than simply an unpleasant side effect. Many experienced participants report that the purge is followed by a shift in the quality of the experience. Resistance to purging often prolongs it; allowing it is generally easier.</p></div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question"><span>Is one ceremony enough?</span><span class="faq-icon">+</span></div>
              <div class="faq-answer"><p>This varies considerably by person and intention. Some people report lasting changes from a single ceremony. Many find that two to three ceremonies within a retreat creates conditions for deeper work. Traditional healing contexts often involve extended series of ceremonies over weeks or months. There is no universal answer, and integration of what arises is at least as important as repeated experience.</p></div>
            </div>

          </div><!-- /.faq-section -->
